{
  "description": "Issue #944, maintainer ruling option 4 (separator lookahead). `and`, `or`, `mod` and `is` are legal dictionary keys, so after a complete entry value a worded operator is ambiguous, and `spec/grammar.md:314`'s Entry lookahead rule settles it by the token that follows -- ignoring line breaks, so the one-line and multi-line spellings of a dictionary always read alike. The discriminator is the separator's LEXEME: a `colon` token opens the next entry (`mod: 2`, `mod : 2`, `or:4`), while a `variable` token is a variable-read and keeps the word an OPERATOR (`mod :two`, `mod:two`). This file was written for #933's superseded rule, under which a newline separated entries and `mod:two`/`mod` + newline + `:two` opened entries keyed `mod`; those cases are re-derived here as single entries, which is the silent reparse the ruling rejected option 2 to avoid. Both readings parse cleanly in every case below, so a wrong reading changes the dictionary with no diagnostic at all -- which is why each entry is read back by name rather than the dict being printed, and why the `:spaced` dictionary is followed by `:one_line`, the same dictionary on one line, whose identical prints are the runtime witness that the two spellings agree. `is :two` belongs to the variable-read group but is deliberately absent: as an operator it is an `is-predicate` missing its form word, so it raises `ol-bad-token` and cannot execute; it is pinned in `packages/parser/src/multiline-expressions.test.mjs`. The last three dictionaries are the scope witnesses -- a word with no separator after it stays an operator, and inside a nested list or group the guard must not leak, each using `mod :two` because it reads BOTH ways without a diagnostic and so detects a leak.",
